so we need the classical\nGarsia–Stanton basis for R n, indexed by elements g ∈ G n. When g = π c 1\n1 · · · π c n\nn , set\nd i(g) = #{j ≥ i : j ∈ Des(g)} for the number of descents at or after position i. The\ndescent monomial b g is defined by\nb g =\nn∏\ni=1\nx rd i(g)+c i\nπ i . (3.4)\nNow, the following set descends to a C-vector space bases for S n,k [CR20, Def. 5.7 &\nThm. 5.8]:\nD n,k = {b g · x ri 1\nπ 1 · · · x ri n−k\nπ n−k : g ∈ G n, des(g) < k, k − des(g) > i 1 ≥ . . . ≥ i n−k ≥ 0}. (3.5)\nFurthermore, R n,k has a similar basis E n,k [CR20, Def. 5.9 & Thm. 5.10] given by all\nelements of the form\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmonomial basis — arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/keyword/monomial%20basis)\nciteturn1search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Plethysm and orbit harmonics ... We determine the standard monomial basis of $R(Π_{n,m})$ with respect to any monomial order, as well as its graded character formula.\nHai Zhu\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nPlethysm and orbit harmonics\n\nLet $Π_{(b^a)}$ be the locus of unordered set partitions of $[ab]$ with $a$ blocks of size $b$. We embed unordered set partitions of $[n]$ into the affine space $\\mathbb{C}^{\\binom{[n]}{2}}$ with coordinate ring $\\mathbb{C}\\Big[\\mathbf{x}_{\\binom{[n]}{2}}\\Big]$. Then, we apply orbit harmonics to $Π_{(2^a)}$ and $Π_{(a^2)}$, yielding graded $\\mathfrak{S}_{2a}$-modules whose graded character formulae respectively refine the Schur expansions of $h_a[h_2]$ and $h_2[h_a]$ according to $λ_1$. We further extend this $λ_1$-separation phenomenon to quotients of $\\mathbb{C}^{\\binom{[n]}{2}}$ where $n$ is odd. Combining $Π_{(b^a)},Π_{(a^b)}$ and orbit harmonics, we propose a conjecture related to Foulkes' conjecture, and we prove the special case $b=2$. We also apply orbit harmonics to the locus $Π_{n,m}$ of unordered set partitions of $[n]$ without blocks of size greater than $m$, yielding a graded $\\mathfrak{S}_n$-module $R(Π_{n,m})$. We determine the standard monomial basis of $R(Π_{n,m})$ with respect to any monomial order, as well as its graded character formula.\n\nmonomial basis plethysm schur schur expansion set partition partition\n\n2025-10-27 v4\n\nJasper M. Liu, Hai Zhu\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nAn extension of Viennot's shadow to rook placements via orbit harmonics\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow Splats Learn (https://taosengine.com/docs/gaussian_splats/index.html)\nciteturn1search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; #### Spherical harmonics: color that changes with viewing angle\n\nA flat-colored blob suits a matte wall, but a metal roof or wet street looks different depending on where you stand. ... Interactive · the spherical-harmonic basis — the fixed patterns a splat's color is built from ... Drag to orbit all spheres together.\n    Y[7] = 0.5462742 * (x*x - y*y);                                         // … (degree 3 = Y[8..14])\n    // backward: each weight's blame is just dL/dcolor · the pattern's value\n    for (var k = 0u; k < activeK; k++) {\n      shGrad[sb + k]          = dRGB.x * Y[k];    // red channel\n      shGrad[sb + SHK + k]    = dRGB.y * Y[k];    // green\n      shGrad[sb + 2u*SHK + k] = dRGB.z * Y[k];    // blue\n    }`\n\nThe backward pass is refreshingly simple: a weight that multiplies a strongly-lit pattern in this view collects proportionally more blame — ∂L/∂sh_{k} = dRGB · Y_{k}. (This particular trainer also lets a sliver of blame flow through the view direction back onto the blob's position, which most implementations skip.) And \"unlocked gradually\" has a precise meaning: only the first activeK = (activeDeg+1)^{2}−1 weights are summed, with activeDeg stepping up by one about every 1000 iterations — inactive patterns contribute nothing and receive no gradient until their band switches on.\n\nInteractive · the spherical-harmonic basis — the fixed patterns a splat's color is built from\n\neach sphere is one basis pattern Yₖ(dir) · ● positive lobe   ● negative lobe  ·  hover a sphere for its formula\n\ndegrees shown [Input] 0–3 the trainer unlocks one band roughly every 1000 steps — learn the plain color first, the glint later. (Degree 0 is the constant, same-from-every-angle term; in this trainer the base color is stored separately as a sigmoid color, and degree ≥1 are the “extra” view-dependent numbers added on top.)\n\nEach blob stores a few numbers — how much of each of these fixed directional patterns to add — so its color can vary smoothly with viewing angle. The low bands set the broad color; higher degrees add finer directional detail (the sharp shine). The trainer switches them on one band at a time, so it learns the plain color before the shine. Drag to orbit all spheres together.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nColored-descent representations of complex reflection groups G(r, p, n) | Israel Journal of Mathematics | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11856-007-0065-z)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11856-007-0065-z\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 234\nL0: Account\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Menu】\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Find a journal】 【2†Publish with us†www.springernature.com】 【3†Track your research†link.springernature.com】\nL5: \nL6: 【4†Search】\nL7: \nL8: 【5†Saved research】\nL9: \nL10: 【6†Cart†order.springer.com】\nL11: # Colored-descent representations of complex reflection groups G(r, p, n)\nL12: \nL13:   * Published: August 2007\nL14: \nL15:   * Volume 160, pages 317–347 (2007)\nL16:   * 【7†Cite this article】\nL17: \nL18: [Input] Save article\nL19: \nL20: 【5†View saved research】\nL21: \nL22: 【8†Israel Journal of Mathematics】 【9†Aims and scope】 Submit manuscript (iton@math.huji.ac.il)\nL23: \nL24: